{"id": "481efbc6-0463-4b14-90f3-c13e7e7073a8", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "Let me look into that for you — checking the lifecycle policy on `nebula-batch-worker` against what's actually still in use by your batch jobs.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_uguskQNRo4cjut0cR1Vjku", "tool_name": "gather_context", "input": {"prompt": "{
  "task": "Determine whether the ECR lifecycle policy on the repository 'nebula-batch-worker' is safe to leave as-is, i.e. whether it would delete any image tag/digest that is still actively in use.",
  "context": "User runs AWS Batch jobs on EC2 that pull images directly from this ECR repository (nebula-batch-worker). They are worried the current lifecycle policy might expire/delete an image that a running or scheduled batch job definition still references. If an ecr-lifecycle-guard skill or similar capability is available to you, use it. Otherwise manually: (1) get the repository's lifecycle policy and simulate/preview which image tags/digests it would expire, (2) cross-reference those candidate images against what is actually in use — AWS Batch job definitions (active revisions) referencing images from this repo, any running/runnable Batch jobs, and any EC2 instances/compute environments currently running containers from these images, (3) flag any overlap between 'would be deleted' and 'currently in use'.",
  "resources": ["ECR repository: nebula-batch-worker"],
  "timeframe": "current state, not historical",
  "criteria": "Specifically identify: (a) the full lifecycle policy rules on nebula-batch-worker, (b) which images/tags would be deleted under that policy right now, (c) whether any of those candidate-for-deletion images are referenced by active/enabled AWS Batch job definitions or currently running Batch jobs/EC2 instances, (d) a clear safe/unsafe verdict with specifics on any at-risk image."
}", "deep_research": false}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Check ECR lifecycle policy on nebula-batch-worker against in-use images"}], "created_at": null}
